r the last 30 years  downstream processes have changed  significantly  traditionally purification processes  consisted of a series of steps which  provided progressive increases in purity  until eventually the desired purity was  achieved however this approach involves  many unit operations which contributes  to the increased costs and time as well  as reduce yields more recently modern  downstream processes are making use of  an affinity capture step to provide a  large increase in purity at the first  step this makes the removal of residual  impurities relatively straightforward by  using one or more polishing steps where  the impurities are captured and the  product passes straight through today is  an example most monoclonal antibodies  are purified using this approach using  immobilized protein a as the affinity  capture step followed by a couple of  polishing steps multimode ligands have  grown to prominence during prominence in  recent years due to their usefulness as  general ligands for capture and  polishing applications these ligands  combine a charge group and a hydrophobic  group into one ligand structure the  example shown here is GE Healthcare's  Capital n MC ligand binding an dilution  is performed by tuning the buffer  conditions to favor protein absorption  or desorption this is achieved by the  addition or removal of salts and/or the  addition or removal of polarity reducing  agents such as alcohols or glycols  however there is currently a significant  limitation to the application of  multimode ligands in that relatively few  of these ligands are currently available  commercially as a consequence buffer and  loading conditions need to be optimized  to suit the ligands available which may  not always be compatible with the  protein in question we believe a better  approach to multimode ligand  chromatography is to produce a diverse  array of multimode ligands and select  the ligand that has the binding and  Ellucian properties compatible with the 